FORTY-FIVE PUBLISHED AREAS, UP FROM THIRTY-TWO

Eight municipalities: Milton, Burlington, Hamilton, Grimsby, St. Catharines,
Niagara-on-the-Lake, Niagara Falls and Barrie. Five communities inside a
municipality: Ancaster, Dundas, Stoney Creek and Waterdown in Hamilton,
Beamsville in Lincoln. Every one of the eighteen Canadian markets in the
first-release brief is now live.

WHAT EACH PAGE SAYS, AND WHAT NONE OF THEM SAYS

Four layers, in the voice the existing fifteen set: what was built here and
when; which floor construction that stock actually takes in this climate; what
the work is on it; and where the market sits on the drive. Hamilton's lower-city
grid gets board loss at removed partitions and radiator penetrations pieced in
before a uniform sand. Burlington gets narrow-strip red oak lifted from under
forty years of broadloom and the thickness measurement above the tongue that
decides whether a full sand is even available. Niagara-on-the-Lake gets heritage
designation constraining what may be changed inside, and the observation that
the fastest route to a bad outcome there is treating an original plank floor as
a subfloor to be levelled. Dundas gets a stone foundation and a damp basement
under the floor being refinished.

signatureProject is undefined on all forty-five, exactly as it was on the first
fifteen. It is the one field that asserts a specific job, and writing one to
make a page feel finished is the failure this repository is arranged to prevent.
docs/PHOTO_QUEUE.md is the shot list that fills it: seven briefs, each naming
the housing type, the floor condition, the two frames, and the sentence already
published that the photograph has to be true to. No page apologises for a
missing photograph, because a live page that does is worse than one that does
not mention it.

A THIRD LIST, BECAUSE THERE ARE THREE KINDS OF PLACE

AREAS are municipalities and become City nodes. NEIGHBOURHOODS are Toronto
sub-areas: pages, never City nodes (F-157). DISTRICTS are communities inside a
municipality other than Toronto, and they needed to exist because Ancaster
arriving in AREAS would have declared a second city inside Hamilton — the same
error F-157 recorded, one municipality over.

placeForArea reads the parent from the record rather than assuming Toronto.
Hard-coding it would have emitted Beamsville as a neighbourhood of Toronto:
invisible on the page, unambiguous to a machine.

THE HIERARCHY WAS WRONG THE MOMENT THE CORRIDOR PUBLISHED

Every published municipality hung from 'gta', which was true while CITIES was
sixteen GTA municipalities and false the instant Hamilton, St. Catharines,
Niagara-on-the-Lake, Niagara Falls, Grimsby and Barrie joined them. None of
those is in the Greater Toronto Area. The region is now read from
ASSESSMENT_MUNICIPALITIES, where it was already recorded once, so a municipality
moving between the published and assessed lists cannot change which region it
is in.

The assessment list also deduplicated by slug alone, and "Niagara Falls" there
slugifies to niagara-falls while the Canadian page is niagara-falls-on — two
Niagara Falls on this corridor, deliberately disambiguated. It would have
published one node and assessed a second for the same place. It now matches on
name as well.

THREE READERS TAUGHT THE THIRD LIST

verify-cities knew two lists and reported five correctly-written entries as
orphans, where the obvious fix is to delete the content rather than teach the
guard. verify-geo and verify-cities both now read AREA_SLUG_OVERRIDES from
seo-data rather than assuming slugify(), so the page-to-market join cannot
disagree with itself. The markdown edition lists communities under their parent
municipality rather than as peers of it.

Two golden queries moved with the facts: Hamilton is a published service area
now, and London took over holding the assessment path under test.

65 guards green, 316 tests, tsc at its 115-error baseline with nothing new.

